CAT = "capswRole"; SEG = "ROL"
# VLO 1 (role, legislation, settings), VLO 2 (accountability, time management), VLO 3 (interprofessional team).
Q = [
("Which document tells a PSW what care to give a specific client?",
 "The client's plan of care", ["The PSW's job description", "The program standard", "The family's wishes list"],
 "The plan of care, developed by the interprofessional team with the client, directs the care a PSW gives. The job description sets the PSW's general duties, not one client's care."),
("Under Ontario law, how are personal support workers classed?",
 "Unregulated care providers", ["Regulated health professionals", "Registered practical nurses", "Members of a health college"],
 "PSWs are not one of the professions regulated under the Regulated Health Professions Act, 1991, so they are unregulated care providers. Registered practical nurses are regulated by the College of Nurses of Ontario."),
("Which body began overseeing registration of Ontario PSWs in December 2024?",
 "HSCPOA", ["College of Nurses of Ontario", "Ontario Health atHome", "Retirement Homes Regulatory Authority"],
 "The Health and Supportive Care Providers Oversight Authority (HSCPOA) started operating on 1 December 2024 and keeps a public register of PSWs. The College of Nurses regulates RNs and RPNs, not PSWs."),
("Which Ontario law lists the controlled acts that only authorized people may perform?",
 "Regulated Health Professions Act", ["Fixing Long-Term Care Act", "Health Care Consent Act", "Occupational Health and Safety Act"],
 "The Regulated Health Professions Act, 1991 sets out the controlled acts, such as giving an injection, and who may perform them. The Health Care Consent Act deals with consent to treatment."),
("A nurse wants to delegate a controlled act to you. What must happen first?",
 "You are taught and found competent", ["The client's family signs a waiver", "You watch the task done once on video", "The client's doctor phones you"],
 "Delegation follows the delegating professional's standards and employer policy: the PSW is taught the task for a specific client and shown to be competent, and stays accountable for doing it correctly. A video or a family waiver is not delegation."),
("A PSW was delegated a task for one client. Can the PSW pass it to a coworker?",
 "No, a PSW cannot re-delegate it", ["Yes, if the coworker is also a PSW", "Yes, if the nurse is busy", "Yes, if the client gives consent to it"],
 "Only the regulated professional who holds the authority can delegate a controlled act, and the person receiving it cannot hand it on. A coworker needs their own teaching and delegation."),
("You are asked to do a task you were never trained for. What should you do?",
 "Tell the supervisor and ask for help", ["Do it carefully as best you can", "Ask the client to talk you through it", "Skip it and chart it as done"],
 "PSWs are accountable for working within their training and role. Telling the supervisor lets the task be reassigned or taught; guessing puts the client at risk, and charting it as done is falsification."),
("Who is a PSW working in a long-term care home usually accountable to?",
 "The registered nursing staff", ["The client's family members", "The home's food services manager", "The Residents' Council"],
 "In long-term care a PSW works under the direction and supervision of registered nursing staff (RNs and RPNs) as set out in employer policy. Families and the Residents' Council have a voice but do not supervise care."),
("A family member asks you to change the time a client's bath is given. What do you do?",
 "Pass it to your supervisor", ["Change it if the client agrees to it", "Tell them it cannot be changed", "Change it and tell no one"],
 "Changes to the plan of care go through the team, so pass the request to your supervisor. The client's preferences matter, but a PSW does not alter the plan on their own."),
("Which worker right under the Occupational Health and Safety Act lets you decline dangerous work?",
 "The right to refuse unsafe work", ["The right to know about hazards", "The right to participate", "The right to file a grievance"],
 "The OHSA gives three main rights: to know, to participate and to refuse unsafe work. Refusing is the one that lets a worker stop a specific task they believe is dangerous while it is investigated."),
("When might the right to refuse unsafe work be limited for a PSW in a care home?",
 "If it would endanger a client's life", ["If the shift is short-staffed", "If the supervisor is off duty that day", "If the client's family objects to it"],
 "The OHSA limits the right to refuse for workers in hospitals, care homes and similar settings when the refusal would directly endanger someone else's life or safety, or when the danger is a normal part of the job. Short staffing alone does not remove the right."),
("You arrive for a home visit and find the client's son drunk and shouting threats. What should you do first?",
 "Leave and call your supervisor", ["Calm him with a firm lecture", "Finish care as fast as you can", "Lock yourself in the bathroom"],
 "Your safety comes first: leave the home, get to a safe place and report to your supervisor, who will arrange care another way. Employers must have workplace violence policies under the OHSA."),
("What is the main purpose of a PSW's time management plan for a shift?",
 "To meet clients' needs in priority order", ["To finish the shift as early as possible", "To give every client equal minutes", "To leave paperwork for the next shift"],
 "Planning the shift around priorities, such as a client who must be up for an appointment or meals on time, makes sure needs are met safely. Equal minutes ignores different needs."),
("Which care setting provides 24-hour nursing care for people who can no longer live at home?",
 "A long-term care home", ["A retirement home", "An adult day program", "A supportive housing unit"],
 "Long-term care homes, governed by the Fixing Long-Term Care Act, 2021, provide 24-hour nursing and personal care. Retirement homes are private residences that sell care services and are governed by the Retirement Homes Act, 2010."),
("Which law governs Ontario's retirement homes?",
 "Retirement Homes Act, 2010", ["Fixing Long-Term Care Act, 2021", "Residential Tenancies Act", "Home Care and Community Services Act"],
 "The Retirement Homes Act, 2010 sets care standards and residents' rights in retirement homes, enforced by the Retirement Homes Regulatory Authority. Long-term care homes fall under the Fixing Long-Term Care Act, 2021."),
("Which law replaced the Long-Term Care Homes Act, 2007 in April 2022?",
 "Fixing Long-Term Care Act, 2021", ["Retirement Homes Act, 2010", "Home Care and Community Services Act", "Health Care Consent Act, 1996"],
 "The Fixing Long-Term Care Act, 2021 came into force on 11 April 2022 and replaced the Long-Term Care Homes Act, 2007. It keeps the Residents' Bill of Rights and the duty to report abuse."),
("You made a mistake giving care and the client was not hurt. What should you do?",
 "Report it to your supervisor now", ["Say nothing since there was no harm", "Fix it quietly next shift", "Ask a coworker to cover it"],
 "Being accountable means reporting errors promptly, even when no harm is seen, so the client can be checked and the cause addressed. Hiding an error can let harm develop unnoticed."),
("Who leads the interprofessional team in a person-centred care model?",
 "The client, as a partner", ["The most senior physician", "The PSW who sees them most often", "The client's eldest child"],
 "Person-centred and person-directed care puts the client at the centre of the team as a partner in decisions. Physicians, nurses, PSWs and families all contribute around them."),
("Which team member usually assesses a client's swallowing problem?",
 "A speech-language pathologist", ["An occupational therapist", "A recreation therapist", "A registered social worker"],
 "Speech-language pathologists assess swallowing and recommend diet textures. Occupational therapists focus on daily activities and equipment, such as adapted cutlery."),
("Which team member would arrange a raised toilet seat and grab bars for a client at home?",
 "An occupational therapist", ["A physiotherapist", "A registered dietitian", "A community pharmacist"],
 "Occupational therapists assess the home and recommend aids that support daily activities. Physiotherapists focus on movement, strength and walking."),
("A coworker keeps skipping clients' mouth care and charting it as done. What should you do?",
 "Report it to your supervisor", ["Do the mouth care for them yourself", "Warn the coworker once more", "Tell the clients' families"],
 "Charting care that was not given is falsification and puts clients at risk, so it must go to the supervisor. Quietly covering for the coworker hides the problem."),
("What does it mean that a PSW is accountable?",
 "Answers for their own actions", ["Paid for every hour worked", "Supervised by a nurse at every task", "Free to change the care plan"],
 "Accountability means a PSW answers for what they do and do not do, including working within their role and reporting concerns. It does not mean being free to change the plan."),
("You find a client's care plan says to walk with a walker, but today they seem unsteady even with it. What should you do?",
 "Keep them safe and report", ["Walk them anyway as planned", "Swap the walker for a cane", "Skip walking from now on"],
 "Follow the plan unless it is unsafe at that moment, keep the client safe (for example, sit them down) and report the change so the team can reassess. A PSW does not change mobility aids on their own."),
("Which statement about self-care for PSWs is correct?",
 "It helps prevent burnout", ["It is a sign of weakness", "It is only for new staff", "It replaces team support"],
 "Caring work is physically and emotionally demanding. Rest, support and stress management help prevent burnout, which harms both the worker and the quality of care."),
("Which is a PSW's role when a client's condition changes?",
 "Observe, report and record it", ["Diagnose the likely cause", "Adjust the client's medication", "Order a test to confirm it"],
 "PSWs observe changes, report them promptly to the responsible person and document them. Diagnosing, adjusting medication and ordering tests belong to regulated professionals."),
("Under the Occupational Health and Safety Act, who must take every reasonable precaution to protect workers?",
 "The employer", ["The client", "The union", "The worker's family doctor"],
 "The OHSA requires employers to take every reasonable precaution for the protection of workers, including training and safe equipment. Workers in turn must follow safe practices and report hazards."),
("A client in their home asks you to drive them to the bank in your own car. Agency policy forbids it. What do you do?",
 "Decline and tell your supervisor", ["Drive them there just this once", "Lend them your own money for a taxi", "Call their bank for them"],
 "Employer policies and job descriptions set the limits of a PSW's work. Decline politely and let the supervisor arrange another option; lending money breaks boundaries."),
("What is the best way to keep your knowledge current as a PSW?",
 "Take ongoing education", ["Rely on what you learned at school", "Copy what senior coworkers do", "Read online forums only"],
 "The program standard expects PSWs to commit to ongoing professional development. Practice and guidelines change, so school learning alone goes out of date."),
]
